changing almost monthly.

48
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:54,410
None of those describe
a particular technology.

49
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:57,640
They describe the job the
runtime needs to perform.

50
00:02:57,770 --> 00:03:00,943
At this point I expected
the decision to become

51
00:03:01,023 --> 00:03:04,710
clearer. Instead another
question kept getting in the way.

52
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:08,400
Almost every AI framework
talks about memory.

53
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:13,010
Conversation memory, working memory,
long-term memory, execution memory.

54
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:16,440
Some treat memory as
something the runtime owns.

55
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:18,980
Others expect it to live elsewhere.

56
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:21,880
The more I read the
more I realized everyone

57
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:23,830
seemed to be using the
same word to describe

58
00:03:23,910 --> 00:03:27,710
several completely different
responsibilities. And if I couldn't

59
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:31,540
explain what memory meant
within Articulate then how could

60
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:34,783
I possibly decide what
runtime would be the better

61
00:03:34,863 --> 00:03:39,300
fit? Because I didn't know which
responsibilities belonged inside

62
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:43,250
the runtime and which belonged
elsewhere in the architecture.

63
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:46,720
So this episode doesn't end
with a technology decision.

64
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:50,860
Instead it marks the beginning
of an investigation captured

65
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:55,840
in ADR 0001. We've identified
the responsibilities at the

66
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:59,410
runtime will eventually need
to fulfil that the runtime

67
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:02,670
is a significant architectural component.

68
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:06,380
Not simply somewhere to execute prompts.

69
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:08,960
And we've uncovered that
the next question needs to

70
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:11,860
be answered before any
technology can be selected.

71
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:15,640
What does memory actually
mean in an AI-native system?

72
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:19,180
Because once we understand
that we'll be much closer

73
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,910
to understanding what
the runtime should own.

74
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:25,640
And only then will we be ready to evaluate

75
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:29,830
the technologies themselves.
That's what we're exploring in the

76
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:32,933
next episode. See you then.
